---
/**
 * Rewrite of casestudy/pepsi.html (2026-08) from owner-supplied project
 * facts. Standard tier. Research credited to the client (tender package) —
 * never first-person. Tender outcome closes the page; no procurement framing
 * up top.
 * Persona portraits (manager/worker/delivery.png) stay orphaned — stock
 * photography, no evidentiary job; files remain in public/ per the URL contract.
 * `worker.mp4` is a portrait clip (478x956); constrained via the same
 * page-local max-width wrapper as before.
 */
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import ContentBlock from '../../components/ContentBlock.astro';
import ContentMedia from '../../components/ContentMedia.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="PepsiCo — supply chain app case study · Jury Alyavdin"
  description="One role-switching app for the people who move PepsiCo's goods from rack to shelf — the design that won the tender, though it was never built."
  path="/casestudy/pepsi.html"
  h1="Designing one app for the three roles that move PepsiCo's goods"
  avatarImage="/casestudy/pepsi/pepsi-logo.png"
  orbitTop="PepsiCo"
  orbitBottom="SaaS • Food company"
  orbitTopOffset="9%"
  orbitBottomOffset="60%"
  glow="#e80828"
  heroImage="/casestudy/pepsi/hero.png"
  heroPosition="left center"
  details={[
    { label: 'Platform', value: 'Web' },
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Product Designer' },
    { label: 'Date', value: '2021' },
  ]}
>
  <ContentBlock title="Overview">
    <p>
      PepsiCo runs its own supply chain: the same company makes the goods, stores them, and trucks
      them to the shops that sell them. I designed the tool meant to run that flow — warehouse
      manager, warehouse worker, delivery manager, one system. I was the solo designer on the bid;
      the commercial side was handled by others, and every flow and screen here is mine. The brief
      covered the existing process end to end, from manufacturing to retail.{' '}
      <strong class="lead-in">The result:</strong> the proposal won PepsiCo's tender. It was never
      built — and the design decisions are the part worth reading.
    </p>
    <p>Manufacturing, distribution and retail were in scope; everything either side of them was somebody else's problem:</p>
    <div class="flow-media">
      <ContentMedia src="/casestudy/pepsi/flow.png" alt="Supply-chain stages with manufacturing, distribution and retail picked out from the rest" width={1520} height={848} />
    </div>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Problem">
    <p>
      The tender package came with PepsiCo's own research: interviews the company had already run
      with its warehouse and delivery staff. I worked from those findings rather than from
      interviews of my own — and they were blunt.{' '}
      <strong class="lead-in">The floor ran on paper:</strong> whatever the existing software said,
      the operation followed printed lists and whiteboards. Any new tool had to beat paper, not the
      old system.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">Dead zones:</strong> the building added a constraint of its own —
      inside the warehouse, connectivity drops between racks, and a tool that assumes a signal will
      stop working exactly where the work happens.
    </p>
    <p>
      One order passed through five pairs of hands on its way to a shelf; three of them — warehouse
      manager, warehouse worker, delivery manager — were the ones the tool had to serve. The
      client's flow map traces the full chain:
    </p>
    <div class="scenario-media">
      <ContentMedia src="/casestudy/pepsi/scenario.png" alt="Swimlane diagram tracing one order across five supply-chain roles, from warehouse to retail" width={2320} height={1306} />
    </div>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="One app, switched by role — not a suite of separate tools">
    <p>
      An order here is a relay: the warehouse manager assembles it, the worker collects it, the
      delivery manager moves it. My first version started where B2B tools usually start — with the
      manager's dashboard. I reversed course once it was clear the floor worker's flow was the
      actual product: if collecting items isn't fast and sure, nothing the dashboards show is
      real.
    </p>
    <p>
      The structural call followed: one app that switches what it shows by who signs in.{' '}
      <strong class="lead-in">What lost:</strong> purpose-built apps per role — the plausible
      default, and the expensive one. Each handoff between separate tools becomes a seam where
      orders stall, and the people sharing one physical flow end up with three systems to learn and
      keep in sync. Inside the shared app, how much each role sees of the others was a decision of
      its own: the delivery manager got a live status view of every order in motion — enough
      oversight to react without reaching into the floor's work.
    </p>
    <p>
      The manager's board survived from that first dashboard-centric draft — now as one face of the
      shared app rather than its center.
    </p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      src="/casestudy/pepsi/manager"
      alt="Kanban-style order board grouping requests, picking, shipment, and delivery stages"
      width={960}
      height={720}
    />
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Offline-first, because the racks kill the signal">
    <p>
      The dead zones between racks weren't an edge case to patch later; they were the normal
      condition of the building. So the worker's flow is offline-first: the job downloads before
      the walk starts, every pick is confirmed locally, and the app reconciles once the signal
      returns. <strong class="lead-in">What lost:</strong> assumed connectivity — the cheaper
      build, and the one the building had already disqualified. A tool that fails between racks
      sends people straight back to printed lists.
    </p>
    <p>
      Scanning became the spine of floor work — point at the code, confirm, move on. Browsing and
      tapping through lists lost: it reads fine in a meeting room and falls apart when your hands
      are full.
    </p>
    <p>
      The worker's screen reduces the whole job to two actions — collect and confirm — designed to
      stay usable where coverage drops:
    </p>
    <div class="worker-media">
      <ContentMedia
        kind="video"
        src="/casestudy/pepsi/worker"
        alt="Phone screen listing items to collect, with product codes and confirmation ticks"
        width={479}
        height={957}
      />
    </div>
  </ContentBlock>

  <ContentBlock title="Outcome">
    <p>
      <strong class="lead-in">Won, never built:</strong> PepsiCo selected this proposal in the
      tender; implementation never followed — designed, chosen, not shipped.
    </p>
    <p>
      The design cost something, too. The delivery manager's clarity was paid for by the warehouse
      worker: live oversight needs live data, and that meant extra confirmations and data entry for
      the person on the floor — the one with the least time to give it.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is the same board the manager sees, opened into a different panel — the visibility the
      floor paid for:
    </p>
    <ContentMedia
      kind="video"
      src="/casestudy/pepsi/delivery"
      alt="Live delivery view with truck loads, a route map, and per-order status"
      width={800}
      height={599}
    />
  </ContentBlock>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .worker-media {
    max-width: 280px;
  }

  /* Both diagrams are dark-UI exports on transparent backgrounds: their ink was
     drawn against the dark canvas, so on the latte theme scenario.png's lane
     labels and flow.png's stage labels drop to 1.5-2.5:1 and effectively
     disappear. A stable dark plate (gray-900 is a primitive, never
     theme-switched) renders both as designed on BOTH themes — seamless on dark,
     a declared dark card on latte. Full fix is an owner re-export with AA
     label contrast. */
  .scenario-media :global(.content-media),
  .flow-media :global(.content-media) {
    background-color: var(--color-gray-900);
  }
</style>
